{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1081 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "56bb1cf1a4e4d2011595342ed179c128f562e69cd6a5259fc54d336aff605a05" :edb/db-before-t 1080 :edb/db-after-t 1081 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534393 50 #inst "2026-10-06T23:58:58.218Z" 13194139534393 true] [17592186183865 1100 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}$." 13194139534393 false] [17592186183865 1100 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}.$" 13194139534393 true] [17592186183865 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos(x){:}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3811/Source/Images/a466fdfbf193e117a9c5e416ee47ffff.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1]$.\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)((\\arccos (x) - π)/(x - π)) &= (\\arccos (0) - π)/(0 - π) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{π}{2} - π))/((- π)) \\\\\n&= ((-\\frac{π}{2}))/((- π)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534393 false] [17592186183865 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\arccos(x){:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi }{x-\\pi }) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)-\\pi }{0-\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi )}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534393 true] [17592186183866 1100 "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$?" 13194139534393 false] [17592186183866 1100 "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)?$" 13194139534393 true] [17592186183866 1145 "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\arccos(x-1){:}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3811/Source/Images/0c8e05c838e67b1eaca00925fab12e20.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2]$. This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined." 13194139534393 false] [17592186183866 1145 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y = \\arccos(x-1){:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2].$ This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined." 13194139534393 true] [17592186183867 1100 "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right).$" 13194139534393 true] [17592186183867 1100 "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right)$." 13194139534393 false] [17592186183867 1145 "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3811/Source/Images/3e8fb5c89782322156fbf09f33393408.png)\n\nThe domain of\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)\n$$\n\nis $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty$, the graph tends to\n\n$$\ny={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)\\cos (\\frac{2}{3}\\arctan (2x + 1)) &= \\cos (\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534393 false] [17592186183867 1145 "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}$  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph tends to $y={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534393 true] [17592186184179 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285932 13194139534393 true] [17592186184179 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285939 13194139534393 true] [17592186184179 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285946 13194139534393 true] [17592186184179 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285974 13194139534393 true] [17592186184179 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285980 13194139534393 true] [17592186184180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285953 13194139534393 true] [17592186184180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285960 13194139534393 true] [17592186184180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285983 13194139534393 true] [17592186184180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285991 13194139534393 true] [17592186184180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285995 13194139534393 true] [17592186184181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285967 13194139534393 true] [17592186184181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285977 13194139534393 true] [17592186184181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285987 13194139534393 true] [17592186184181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285998 13194139534393 true] [17592186184460 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534393 true] [17592186184460 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$" 13194139534393 true] [17592186184460 1100 "What is $lim_(x → - 1/2)\\tan (\\frac{1}{2}\\arccos (x))$?" 13194139534393 false] [17592186184460 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534393 true] [17592186285932 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285933 13194139534393 true] [17592186285932 1089 "q-152757" 13194139534393 true] [17592186285932 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534393 true] [17592186285932 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)).$" 13194139534393 true] [17592186285932 1113 #uuid "78b11030-53ab-5210-86a9-a47f5842bef6" 13194139534393 true] [17592186285932 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534393 true] [17592186285933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285934 13194139534393 true] [17592186285933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285935 13194139534393 true] [17592186285933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285936 13194139534393 true] [17592186285933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285937 13194139534393 true] [17592186285933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285938 13194139534393 true] [17592186285933 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534393 true] [17592186285933 1041 "selection" 13194139534393 true] [17592186285933 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285938 13194139534393 true] [17592186285933 1110 #uuid "a6dd5e83-24c1-5120-bd45-0ede9e9579cc" 13194139534393 true] [17592186285934 1034 "-2\\pi " 13194139534393 true] [17592186285934 1103 #edb/r